## 4. File Change Summary Table

| File Path | Action | Component / Section | Change Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `src/store/chats.js` | Modify | module helper `isActiveProjectChat` | New helper telling whether a chat is owned by the **currently active** project (falls back to the previous behaviour when the owner can't be determined). |
| `src/store/chats.js` | Modify | `actions.reloadChat` | No longer **registers** a freshly loaded chat into the store unless it belongs to the active project (merge path untouched). |
| `src/store/chats.js` | Modify | `actions.onChatEvent` | Bails out early for socket chat events whose chat is not in the store **and** belongs to another project, so cross-project saves (board import) can't leak cards into the current board. |

### Why the cards were duplicated (and why a refresh fixed it)
- Copying/moving a board calls `PUT /api/chats?chat_only=0` for every task in the **target** project.
- The backend emits a `chat_event` (`event_type: "changed"`) for each save; the broadcast reaches this client.
- `session.onEvent` forwards it to `chats.onChatEvent`; since the new chat id wasn't in the store, `reloadChat` downloaded it and `registerChat`ed it into the **active** project's store.
- An imported board keeps its title (no collision), so its tasks matched `c.board === <current board id>` → the current board rendered them as **duplicates**.
- On the next reload the store is rebuilt from the active project only → duplicates gone.

Guarding both the event handler and the registration path keeps the chats store strictly scoped to the active project (consistent with the `importChat` guard), while live updates for the active project's chats continue to work.

